[Music]  President Trump is expected to meet with  members of law enforcement later today  now this comes after protesters flooded  the streets again over the weekend  demanding an end to police brutality and  the defunding of police forces I spoke  with the heads of four police  departments ATS Dallas Police Chief  Renee Hall Santa Cruz police chief  Andrew Mills Camden New Jersey Police  Chief Joseph Wysocki and last but  certainly not least Raleigh Police Chief  Casandra dick Brown so we discussed many  things including the flaws in the system  and the need for change but we started  with the reaction to the video of George  Floyd's death I was angry at the  profession because it took me back to  why police saying even exist in the  first place  police officers exist because of slave  patrols so there's something that goes  back to that one of the things that was  so disturbing is that it seems so  Cavalier and so cruel on some level he  must have felt comfortable doing what he  did because in the past there doesn't  appear to have been consequences for  their actions okay all I think you're  right I think it's a fairly small  percentage but it's a significant  percentage of people who feel empowered  and the mere fact that the other  officers didn't intercede it is they're  just as guilty if you see it you own it  I agree it's the one one thing I think  we have to stress the duty to intervene  we need to stress stress that do you  think most police officers feel  comfortable reporting bad behavior from  another officer yeah you know I think it  is tough to turn out the cops in because  you depend on them for your life and  they make sometimes make poor decisions  and you're going to try to do what you  can to protect them but we've got to get  past that generally speaking what could  I have a show of hands do you think that  black people are treated differently  than white people by the police in this  country  that was a unanimous that was a  unanimous I think there are differences  and they are systemic it's community  based as well it's not just in law  enforcement and how many of us have been  in a store and people have walked around  while you're shopping being because of  the color of your skin and I think now  is that time to really pay attention to  where we are and to really be a part of  the change here's the reality for me I'm  a 63 year old white guy from a new class  family with an education I'm privileged  nobody's gonna kneel on my neck I would  guess 4 percent of our police agencies  shouldn't be there but those are the 4  percent that we've got to get rid of you  have to figure out how to do that  that is where the racism occurs I think  we need to take a look at how unions are  empowered to protect the officers  immediately the police union there said  don't condemn the officer and don't rush  to judgment and I think that's another  thing that bothers people that even when  we see something that appears to be so  blatant it's still wait a second don't  rush to judgment let there be an  investigation you know some of the  issues that have come up have been that  same issue is that police association  coddled officers and they make their  behavior acceptable do you think that's  true do you think that's true absolutely  I do I think in in some instances there  are Association members who regardless  of the behavior of an officer we blame  everything else this is not the first  case where an unarmed black man was  killed where people think unfairly  unjustified this feels different to me  does it to you yeah Gail it does feel  different to me it shocked the  consciousness of the profession is  shallow symbolism of just a one time  speaking out we can't stop here and I  mean where were we when some of these  other people were killed there was a lot  of silence from our profession and for  that we are wrong I am wrong I am sorry  why do you think Chiefs didn't speak up  before it had to be something this  heinous chief all the  positions or in if we're going to be  truthful about it our very political and  so I think the balance of what is true  and right versus what is politically  correct to say gets in the way but I  believe that we're sick and tired of  being sick and tired as a nation because  we see that their systemic change that  has to take place in order for us to be  on the other side of this and I think  what we're seeing now as a result of the  pain of the hurt of the tired is a  result of not having that seat at the  table but weren't you all aware of the  pain the hurt and the tired to use your  words chief Hall before the murder of  George Floyd we've seen it in all of our  nothing change and nothing changed so  when we say nothing's changed I don't  think we're being you know genuine in  that the fact that things have changed  but he may not have done a good enough  job of informing them that these  recommendations have come in and we  implemented them I want to talk to you  all about the protest chief Wysocki  President Obama singled your department  out for the way that you handle  protesters so what are you doing that  the rest of the country could learn from  we weren't in riot gear we weren't and  you have a helmet on or like the big big  sticks we just read on regular uniform  you know we marched and you have to  continually be doing community policing  you have to do community outreach and  you have to listen chief Rosa you made  national news for kneeling with the  protesters and their demonstrators we  stand for justice and we stand for  making sure that racial and justices are  dealt with and so it's not just the  black community speaking out you have a  lot of community speaking out but now we  have to follow through with real policy  changes legislative changes training and  I hope if there's leadership in  Washington to ensure that this takes  place in a thoughtful way and in a rapid  way that was Andy Mills listen all of  them were very good talkers I found Andy  Mills to be  especially forthcoming in his thoughts  they all agree that leadership that  change starts at the top and leadership  is key and there they have to be the  ones that initiate that to at least  start the conversation they also  suggested that maybe it's time for us  all to take a look at police unions and  the power that police unions have when  you look at Officer Chauvin who had 18  complaints against him and only two  written reprimands and that he was still  on the force was troubling to all of  them  I also Anthony want to point out that we  had that conversation before there was  all of the talk about defunding the  police departments because I'm thinking  why didn't you ask about that well it  hadn't happened when we had that  conversation I would have been very  curious to get their thoughts on that  there's still a fascinating conversation  with those four Chiefs kale and I was I  was particularly struck by the one who  said that that what is it's things have  been tolerated in the police department  in part because they've been tolerated  in society so it you know with all this  focus on the police but it's it's a  larger issue I really it was a very  interesting conversation yeah and they  admit this feels different tomorrow  night I will anchor the CBS primetime  special we're calling it justice for all  it's at 10 o'clock 9:00 Central right  here on CBS it explores how the activism  that we're all seeing right now  following the death of George Floyd  could change racism within the ranks of  law enforcement in the country forever

